DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20270114T140000
SUMMARY:Jan 14 - MCP\, Agents and Skills Meetup
DESCRIPTION:Raleigh AI\, Machine Learning and Computer Vision Meetup\nJoin
  our virtual meetup to hear talks from experts on cutting-edge topics acro
 ss AI\, ML\, and computer vision.\n\n**Date\, Time and Location**\n\nJan 1
 4\, 2027\n9:00 AM - 11:00 AM PST\nOnline. **[Register for the Zoom!](https
 ://voxel51.com/events/mcp-agents-skills-meetup-january-14-2027)**\n\n**Fro
 m Demo to Production: Testing\, Scaling and Securing AI Agents with Spring
  AI**\n\nYour agent works perfectly in the demo. Then in production it giv
 es a wrong answer and nobody can tell why\, token costs spike\, it slows t
 o a crawl under load\, and it calls a tool the user was never allowed to u
 se. Building an agent is the easy part\; the real work is running it like 
 any other production service: tested\, observable\, scalable and secure.\n
 \nSpring AI lets you do that with the Spring idioms and tooling you alread
 y know. This code-first session grows one example step by step. We start w
 ith a simple ChatClient\, add advisors\, memory and RAG\, comparing the op
 tions Spring AI offers for each\, and then let the agent act through tool 
 calling and MCP.\n\nThen come the production questions:\n\n* Testing: we c
 atch regressions with LLM-as-judge evaluators and typed decision models li
 ke Jev for fast\, cheap verdicts.\n* Observability: we add Micrometer trac
 es\, logs and token usage across model and tool calls.\n* Scaling: we comp
 are options for handling more concurrent users\, from self-hosting to a ma
 naged runtime such as Amazon Bedrock AgentCore.\n* Security: we use Spring
  Security to authenticate users and make sure only the MCP tools they're a
 llowed to use ever get called. You'll leave with reusable Spring patterns 
 for agents that reason and act\, and the discipline to test\, observe\, sc
 ale and secure them in production.\n\n*About the Speaker*\n\n[Andrei Shaki
 rin](https://www.linkedin.com/in/andrei-shakirin/) is a Solutions Architec
 t at Amazon Web Services and a former Pivotal Labs engineer\, specializing
  in the design of highly scalable systems and generative AI applications. 
 He is an active committer to several Spring and Apache open-source project
 s\, contributor into MCP java SDK and a regular speaker at technical confe
 rences.\n\n**From Model Evals to System Evals: Evaluating Agents\, Harness
 es\, and the Evaluators Themselves**\n\nEvery team shipping agents is quie
 tly standing up two systems: the agent harness that plans\, retrieves\, ca
 lls tools\, and edits code — and the evaluation harness that decides whe
 ther any of it can be trusted. Most teams only evaluate the first one's fi
 nal output.\n\nThat is not enough. An agent can produce a correct answer t
 hrough an unreliable\, wasteful\, or out-of-policy execution path\, and ou
 tcome-only checks will pass it. This talk argues for evaluating the whole 
 agent system: execution trajectories — tool correctness\, evidence and g
 rounding\, state handling\, policy compliance\, and efficiency — alongsi
 de final outcomes\, using deterministic specification checks an agent cann
 ot game\, model-based judges\, and human-calibrated benchmarks.\n\nWe sepa
 rate capability evaluations from regression evaluations so harness or mode
 l changes can be gated in CI. Then we turn the lens around: the evaluators
  themselves need evaluating — LLM judge calibration\, disagreement analy
 sis\, and where human review still belongs.\n\nAttendees leave with a prod
 uction checklist for gating agent\, model\, and harness changes on system-
 level evidence instead of final answers.\n\n*About the Speaker*\n\n[Dipen 
 Chawla](https://www.linkedin.com/in/dipen-chawla-bb4a49bb/) is an AI engin
 eer and researcher working on agentic AI systems and their evaluation\, in
 cluding agent memory and clinical diagnostic reasoning work at Microsoft\,
  and data platform engineering at Walmart and S&P Global.\n\n**Leading an 
 Army of AI Agents**\n\nOur AI coding agents now pick up tasks\, follow tea
 m playbooks\, open pull requests and wait for review. This firsthand engin
 eering case study follows more than ten agents running in parallel and sho
 ws how briefs\, shared context and verification loops keep their work revi
 ewable.\nIt covers the failure modes too: confident wrong turns\, incomple
 te verification\, review bottlenecks and the attention required from human
  leaders.\nAttendees will learn a practical task-to-PR workflow\, how to m
 ake trust conditional on evidence\, and which measures expose the costs hi
 dden by faster output. The focus is on agent workflow and operating discip
 line\, with concrete engineering examples rather than a product pitch.\n\n
 *About the Speaker*\n\n[John Adib](https://www.linkedin.com/in/MrAdib) is 
 a London-based engineering manager at Zapp\, a former CTO and two-time fou
 nder with 18+ years of software experience.\n\nAgents in Action: Putting A
 I Agents to Work on Visual AI Data\n\nWhat should an AI agent remember\, a
 nd what should it forget? In this talk\, we’ll explore the Voxel51 agent
  through the lens of memory\, examining how retaining the right context an
 d incorporating feedback can help agents improve over time. Through exampl
 es from visual AI workflows\, we’ll discuss what’s worth remembering\,
  from dataset context and user preferences to corrections and successful a
 pproaches\, and when to update or discard information that becomes outdate
 d. We’ll also explore how feedback and evaluation can help us determine 
 whether an agent is making better decisions\, offering practical insights 
 for building agents that become more useful with experience.\n\n*About the
  Speaker*\n\n[Adonai Vera](https://www.linkedin.com/in/adonai-vera/) is a 
 Machine Learning Engineer and DevRel at Voxel51 with over 7 years of exper
 ience building computer vision and machine learning models using TensorFlo
 w\, Docker\, and OpenCV.
